# Clear language (Documentation Standard)

Applies when creating, editing, or reviewing Markdown under this framework.

Goal: a new reader (engineer, operator, or next-session agent) understands on the **first read**.

## Rules

1. Prefer **direct, technical prose**. Follow [docs/en/style-guide.md](docs/en/style-guide.md) (pt-BR: [docs/pt-BR/style-guide.md](docs/pt-BR/style-guide.md)).
2. **Code identifiers stay in English** (paths, APIs, env names, status tokens). In human prose, put the identifier in backticks and state the meaning in the same sentence.
3. Do not use jargon alone on first appearance in a section — define it once (or link the glossary).
4. Numbers need units; lists that matter must be complete or marked Unknown / not applicable.
5. One idea per section; prefer tables and lists over long narrative.

## Ambiguity check (before closing a doc edit)

- [ ] Abbreviations / anglicisms defined or linked
- [ ] Claims use Confirmed / Inferred / Unknown / Proposed when classification applies
- [ ] No secrets or PII in examples — placeholders only
- [ ] Canonical names reused (no synonym drift) — [document-identity.md](docs/en/document-identity.md)

## Anti-patterns

- Marketing tone or unmeasurable adjectives ("robust", "seamless") without metrics
- Leaving knowledge only in chat while the doc stays vague
- Inventing synonyms for the same component or contract

